WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.640
MUSIC

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:34.720
This white wilderness, this emptiness, is the North Pole.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.800
I'm standing in the middle of a frozen ocean.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:46.520
Beneath my feet and for over 500 miles in every direction, there are several metres

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.800
of ice.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:55.120
But something significant is likely to happen here at the North Pole soon.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:03.000
Chances are that sometime within the next few decades, perhaps even as soon as 2020,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:11.160
there will be open water here, for the first time in human recorded history.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.760
The Arctic and Antarctic are changing.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.920
Enormous masses of ice that have been frozen for thousands of years are breaking apart

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:35.160
and melting away.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:40.880
Ice scientists are going to extremes to find out exactly what's going on.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.400
For them, these are exciting times.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:56.520
But the transformation that's being seen here will be felt far beyond the polar wilderness.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:02.920
In this programme, I'll be trying to understand what these changes mean, not just to the wildlife

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:19.120
and people that live around the poles, but to the whole planet.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:27.880
I'm starting my journey in the Arctic, the far north of our planet.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:33.080
It's still very cold outside, by most people's standards, but the Arctic has been warming

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:37.840
fast, twice as fast as the rest of our planet.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:44.240
My first mission is to find out what effect that's having on the animals, although first,

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.240
we have to find them.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:46.240
It's April in Svalbard.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.240
We're a thousand miles north the Arctic Circle in search of the region's top predator.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:03:08.240
We need to travel away from the land and out over the frozen sea.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:23.240
There's some tracks right beneath us.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:29.360
Over there.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:38.600
I'm with a Norwegian team which is giving the polar bears of Svalbard their yearly health

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:39.600
check.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:40.600
She's under us now.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:49.360
I'll come round for a clean shot.

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.920
The team works together to give an anaesthetic injection from a dart gun without hurting

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.120
the bear.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:22.960
It takes tremendous skill.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.680
Nobody likes to see a magnificent animal like a polar bear lolling about unconscious on

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:35.120
the ice, but it's only by darting them in this way and keeping check on them year after

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.440
year that we can be sure we know what is happening to them and the population of polar bears

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.880
as a whole.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.840
Over the last 30 years, many teams have been seeing the condition of their local bears

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:55.240
deteriorate, although not every bear is suffering.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:00.960
How much?

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.600
96 here.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:08.600
And 102 here.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:09.600
197.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.600
Is that good?

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:13.600
It's not too bad.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:14.600
It's a bit above average.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:18.760
So she's a bear in a good condition for Svalbard to be.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:24.720
The trouble is that if this was underweight, she would be in trouble.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.840
Not only from her own point of view, but from the point of view of our cubs, because an

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:36.240
underweight female gives birth to underweight cubs and underweight cubs have a great problem

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:42.880
of surviving their difficult first year in these circumstances.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:48.360
It can be minus 40 degrees centigrade when polar bear cubs emerge at the start of the

00:05:48.360 --> 00:06:04.320
Arctic Spring from their dens where they were born.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:09.480
This mother hasn't eaten for half a year.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:14.160
She and her cubs need to fatten up fast over the next few months, and their chances of

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:21.880
survival depend on what's happening beneath their feet.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:25.080
These polar bears aren't walking on land.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:32.160
They're roaming across the frozen surface of the sea.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:48.200
And the bears' food lives under the ice.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:52.040
Ring seals are hunted by polar bears.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:59.640
In fact, in some parts, polar bears eat almost nothing else.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:07.760
So it's very understandable that this mother ring seal is looking at me now.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:11.160
She's a little apprehensive.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:20.200
That pup of hers is only about three or four days old.

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:27.320
And the pup won't be able to swim for another two or three days.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:31.120
Polar bears have good reason to be nervous around their holes.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:39.660
They need the holes to breathe when the sea is frozen, but this makes them easy to find.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:50.800
Polar bears can sniff out seal holes even if they're covered in snow.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:55.740
Spring is the best hunting season.

00:07:55.740 --> 00:08:03.560
These mothers found a food store under the snow that was probably made by an arctic fox.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:08.400
It's a time of plenty now, but the bear family need to make the best of it because the good

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:16.460
times are about to come to an end.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:22.840
As the weather warms, the ice beneath the bears' feet starts to break up and then melt.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:32.040
And as the ice dwindles, so do the bears' chances of a successful hunt.

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:42.960
Most of the ice is lost over the shallow coastal waters where most of the seals live.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:46.200
It's now summer and these bears have a choice.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:56.480
Take their chances on the shrinking ice flows or make for the safety of the land.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:06.640
It's a case of sink or swim.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:10.380
Bears have always gone hungry in the summer, but the length of time when there's enough

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:19.200
ice for them to go hunting is getting shorter and shorter across much of the arctic.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:25.280
This is hitting cubs particularly hard because they can't survive for as long without feeding

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:28.840
as their mother.

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:42.300
Bears that were born underweight are at the greatest risk.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:47.280
This mother and her cubs may well not get another meal until the sea freezes again in

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.800
winter.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:54.400
There's not much to eat on land and the fact is that the longer the cubs have to wait until

00:09:54.400 --> 00:10:01.640
the ice returns, the more likely they are to die.

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:07.520
Longer summers with no ice are probably the main reason why many polar bear populations

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:16.840
are dropping.

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:21.680
To help monitor bears into the future, this female is being fitted with a radio collar

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:27.760
to track her movements.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:33.960
It's an extraordinary sensation to be so close to such a powerful animal.

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:41.720
With luck carrying that collar, she will have more years to go yet and be telling us a great

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:50.160
deal about herself and the rest of the race of polar bears as they face this very uncertain

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:58.400
future.

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:04.020
The future of the ice cover on the sea isn't just an issue for the animals.

00:11:04.020 --> 00:11:08.800
It's a big concern for the people who live in the arctic and travel across the ice every

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:17.920
day.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:26.160
David Ikakwaru is an Inuit from the village of Clyde River in the Canadian Far North.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:35.520
There are very few roads up here so David and his community, like most Inuit people,

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:40.920
have always travelled across the frozen sea.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:46.440
Dog sleds are the safest way to get around because the dogs feel thin ice underfoot and

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:51.280
won't lead travellers into trouble.

00:11:51.280 --> 00:12:00.800
Old timers like David know the ice as well as we know the streets in our local neighbourhood.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:06.840
Every spring, cracks have always formed in the same places at the same time.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.000
It's going to be big very soon.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:11.840
After two weeks maybe.

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:16.760
We'll be more open.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:22.680
But now cracks are appearing where they never did before so David and his friend Limeiki

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:26.400
have taken on a new job.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:35.380
They are using special GPS units to record the position of new cracks or weak ice.

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:39.840
These findings will be used by locals for their own safety but they're also being studied

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:53.900
by ice scientists who want to predict how the ice will change in years to come.

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:58.040
The Inuit are keen to know what the future holds too because they've seen with their

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:04.380
own eyes the changes that the scientists have seen from space.

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:10.040
This satellite photo from 1980 shows the Arctic Ocean at the end of the summer when ice cover

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:12.040
is at its minimum.

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:19.440
Since then there's been a 30% drop in the area covered by ice.

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:25.840
But these images can't tell us about changes to the most important factor, the thickness

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:30.800
of the ice.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:36.920
Ice thickness across the whole ocean was beyond scientists for many years until help came

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:56.160
from an unexpected source.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:02.600
The Arctic Ocean is of huge military importance as it's the shortest route between North America

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:09.600
and Russia.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:15.280
Since the late 1950s British, US and Russian submarines have been patrolling the Arctic

00:14:15.280 --> 00:14:19.420
Ocean.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:23.820
But as well as looking out for enemy activity they've also been measuring the thickness

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:31.440
of the ice, critical when looking for a place to surface.

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:35.760
When scientists got permission to look at the submarine crew's records they discovered

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:38.300
that the ice has been thinning fast.

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:46.920
In fact it's nearly halved in thickness since 1980.

00:14:46.920 --> 00:14:56.100
Across most of the Arctic Ocean there are now just a couple of metres of ice.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:01.060
It's so thin that it could melt away almost entirely in the summertime and that includes

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:04.280
the ice at the North Pole.

00:15:04.280 --> 00:15:09.800
If current trends continue then there will be open ocean here by summer's end sometime

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:14.740
within the next few decades.

00:15:14.740 --> 00:15:21.360
So the days of the Arctic Ocean being covered by a continuous sheet of ice seem to be past.

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:30.040
Whether or not that's a good or bad thing of course depends on your point of view.

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:34.640
Nobody has had a better view of the changes to the Arctic Ocean than the people of Barrow,

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:37.480
the most northerly town in Alaska.

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:42.800
The people here have always survived by hunting on the frozen sea and they celebrate this

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:45.480
at a festival every year.

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:51.040
The blanket toss was once the best way to spot distant animals to hunt as lifelong

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:54.360
resident Lewis Brower explains.

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:58.760
When we throw ourselves up into the blanket you know you get that much more of an awe

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:04.880
of seeing further and further out so sometimes you'll jump 15, 20 feet in the air and hopefully

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:08.080
you're being caught right back into the blanket.

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:13.480
I'm okay!

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:15.960
But the old way of life is under threat.

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:21.620
When Lewis was young the seas stayed frozen to the horizon until July and some ice remained

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:23.040
offshore all summer.

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:29.120
But now it's breaking up in June and melting away completely for two or three months.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:36.840
I used to go out on ice all the time this time of the year but we can't do that anymore

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:40.800
because there's no more ice.

00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:48.240
Lewis can also see that the loss of sea ice is affecting the animals he hunts for a living.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:53.760
Since 2007 something very strange has been happening on this stretch of coastline close

00:16:53.760 --> 00:16:58.840
to Barrow.

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:06.180
Mother walruses confused by the lack of ice are crowding onto the land with their pups.

00:17:06.180 --> 00:17:10.960
This very tight crowding isn't normal and it's caused many youngsters to be crushed

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:14.820
to death.

00:17:14.820 --> 00:17:19.640
Many Arctic animals are threatened by the changing conditions and that's also bad news

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:22.200
for the traditional hunters.

00:17:22.200 --> 00:17:27.040
But the ice loss could be good news for some people.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:35.640
There are trillions of dollars worth of oil and gas under the Arctic Ocean but the only

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:42.680
way to get to them until now has been by building expensive artificial islands like this.

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:48.400
But if the sea ice goes it will be much easier to drill for the huge riches below so the

00:17:48.400 --> 00:18:04.120
countries that surround the Arctic are scrambling to stake their claims.

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:11.280
This daring attempt by the Russians to claim the disputed seabed at the North Pole in 2007

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:19.120
caused fury among the competing countries and it's unlikely to be the last such dispute.

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:27.260
The Arctic has never been so important and not just because of its resources.

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:32.360
The Northwest Passage, a legendary sea route around the north of Canada and Alaska, cleared

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:38.560
of ice in the summer of 2007 for the first time since records began.

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:48.840
This promises a much faster and cheaper shipping route between the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans.

00:18:48.840 --> 00:18:57.960
And some wildlife could benefit from an ice-free Arctic too.

00:18:57.960 --> 00:19:03.160
Bowhead whales are one of just a few whales that can live year round in the Arctic because

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:07.200
they have no dorsal fin.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:14.120
This means they can come up for air in small spaces and travel easily under the ice.

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:18.740
Their unique body shape used to mean that the Arctic whales have the seas to themselves

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:20.240
for most of the year.

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:32.040
But now some cousins from down south are moving in.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:36.600
Killer whales are now a much more common sight in the Arctic.

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:41.360
Their tall fins make it difficult for them to travel under ice, but the longer summers

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:54.360
mean they can travel much farther north and make the most of the rich Arctic seas.

00:19:54.360 --> 00:20:09.160
For animals and people, it will be those who can adapt who will thrive in a changing Arctic.

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:13.440
But the loss of sea ice isn't just an issue for the Arctic because the state of the ice

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:19.680
affects the climate of the whole planet.

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:24.960
Because it's white, the ice reflects up to 90% of the sun's energy.

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:30.120
This is called the albedo effect, and it's why we often see heat haze in the Arctic even

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:38.800
when the air feels cold.

00:20:38.800 --> 00:20:46.640
The frozen Arctic Ocean acts as a huge reflector, bouncing back the sun's heat into space.

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:49.720
Throughout history, that has helped to cool the planet.

00:20:49.720 --> 00:20:59.560
But when the ice melts, it's a different story.

00:20:59.560 --> 00:21:05.240
Because sea water is dark, it absorbs most of the sun's heat.

00:21:05.240 --> 00:21:11.320
In the Arctic, this can trigger a chain reaction as the warming water melts more ice, exposing

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:19.040
more water to the sun's heat.

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:24.200
This cycle of warming as huge areas start to absorb rather than reflect heat is the

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:30.000
main reason why the Arctic, a region the size of North America, is warming twice as fast

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:36.680
as the rest of the Earth.

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:39.560
So melting sea ice is a big issue.

00:21:39.560 --> 00:21:45.540
But there's another kind of ice that could have an even more dramatic impact on our world.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:50.640
The ice that is found on land.

00:21:50.640 --> 00:22:02.940
This is freshwater ice, formed from thousands of years of accumulated snowfall.

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:09.000
This is the front of a glacier, quite a small one, believe it or not.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:15.380
Glaciers are like rivers of frozen freshwater flowing across the surface of the land.

00:22:15.380 --> 00:22:23.000
This one, like most polar glaciers, is flowing down from a vast inland ice sheet.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:28.520
And it's what happens to those ice sheets that could radically alter the face of the

00:22:28.520 --> 00:22:30.740
planet.

00:22:30.740 --> 00:22:34.520
The Greenland ice sheet is by far the largest in the Arctic.

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:47.080
It's two miles thick in places and six times the size of the United Kingdom.

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:53.080
Every summer, some of the surface of the ice sheet melts, forming sapphire blue lakes of

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:56.320
melt water.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:23:03.520
More and more of these lakes have been forming as Greenland has warmed over the last 20 years.

00:23:03.520 --> 00:23:08.560
This lake has grown over several weeks and now it's overflowing, carving a deep channel

00:23:08.560 --> 00:23:11.560
through the ice.

00:23:11.560 --> 00:23:22.480
A network of channels crisscrosses the ice sheet, but many of them come to an abrupt

00:23:22.480 --> 00:23:29.480
end.

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:51.240
Huge holes like this can open up quite suddenly, draining the melt water away.

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:56.760
Alan Hubbard is a glaciologist, studying the enormous power of these waterfalls, which

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:57.760
are known as moolands.

00:23:57.760 --> 00:24:03.720
We've got this amazing mooland going off here today.

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:07.920
The water is overflowing from the lake, which is beginning to drain.

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:15.040
Tons of water cascading down this pipe that is effectively plummeting to the depths of

00:24:15.040 --> 00:24:26.880
the ice sheet through over a kilometer of vertical ice.

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:31.960
Alan is here to study where the melt water goes and what effect it has on the remaining

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:33.240
ice.

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:44.240
To do that, he needs to find a mooland that has recently run dry.

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:50.320
Just a week ago, there was a three mile long, 10 meter deep lake here.

00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:55.040
The weight of all that water cramped the ice beneath and the lake drained in just a few

00:24:55.040 --> 00:24:59.240
hours with incredible force.

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:13.960
A thousand ton ice boulders were tossed about like dice.

00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:17.840
Alan's team have found the hole down which the lake disappeared and they want to have

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:18.840
a closer look.

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:29.240
It's not a job for anyone with a fear of heights.

00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:33.200
As you can see, it's dry up here, but if you listen, you can hear the thunder of there's

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:37.960
a lot of water entering it at some depth.

00:25:37.960 --> 00:25:43.400
Alan wants to place a sensor deep into the mooland to discover how much water is flowing

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:50.760
through the ice.

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:54.360
As they drop, they travel back in time.

00:25:54.360 --> 00:26:01.680
30 meters down and they reach ice formed from snow that fell 10,000 years ago in the last

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:05.780
ice age.

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:13.560
When this lake drained and the plug got pulled and the whole lot flushed down through here,

00:26:13.560 --> 00:26:19.960
this ice sheet, it rose by a meter as that water accessed the bed and force jacked up

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:21.140
the ice sheet.

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:27.680
So we know that the water in this whole plumbing cavity system down here, we know that shoots

00:26:27.680 --> 00:26:32.120
straight through the ice and actually hits the bed of the ice sheet.

00:26:32.120 --> 00:26:36.720
We can see the water now.

00:26:36.720 --> 00:26:37.720
Great.

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:40.800
Nice work.

00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:45.740
This daring experiment is measuring how the water flowing under the ice sheet affects

00:26:45.740 --> 00:26:50.640
the speed with which the glaciers flow from it down to the sea.

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:55.440
The theory is that the water is acting as a lubricant, so the more water there is, the

00:26:55.440 --> 00:26:59.920
faster the glacier flows.

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:08.040
To the naked eye, glaciers don't appear to move at all, but move they do.

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:23.680
These unique time lapse images were captured over the last four years.

00:27:23.680 --> 00:27:29.520
Through long observations, we now know that Greenland's ice is flowing down to the sea

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:34.320
twice as quickly as it was 20 years ago.

00:27:34.320 --> 00:27:39.040
The speed of the glaciers affects our sea levels because when they reach the water,

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:41.600
they break apart into icebergs.

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:51.080
Occasionally, a real mega-berg is born.

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:57.840
This is the straw glacier in May 2010.

00:28:51.080 --> 00:29:00.080
75 million tons of ice that have been sitting on land for thousands of years has broken

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:06.000
away.

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:11.000
Events like this have become increasingly common as Greenland's glaciers flow faster

00:29:11.000 --> 00:29:12.000
into the sea.

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:32.200
Every single one of these icebergs raises the sea level a small amount.

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:36.080
Scientists monitoring the ice sheet predict that Greenland might add as much as a half

00:29:36.080 --> 00:29:41.920
meter to world sea levels by the end of the century, enough to swamp many of the world's

00:29:41.920 --> 00:29:55.440
low-lying islands.

00:29:55.440 --> 00:30:10.600
99% of the Arctic's freshwater ice is in Greenland.

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:15.960
It's a staggeringly big ice sheet, but it's just a drop in the ocean compared to that

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:26.880
at the southern end of our planet.

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:34.080
In Antarctica, there is 10 times more ice, by far the largest concentration of ice on

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:40.000
Earth.

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:50.600
Our exploration of the Antarctic only began a little over 100 years ago.

00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:56.160
The study of ice retreat here was unwittingly begun on an expedition led by the great early

00:30:56.160 --> 00:31:03.280
explorer Ernest Shackleton.

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:10.320
In 1916, after their expedition boat was crushed and sunk by ice, Shackleton and two companions

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:17.000
set off to summon help in a tiny boat.

00:31:17.000 --> 00:31:22.560
They sailed over 800 miles across the southern ocean to the island of South Georgia on the

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:28.240
edge of the Antarctic.

00:31:28.240 --> 00:31:33.120
Near starving and dressed in rags, the three men walked across the ice sheet at the center

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:37.640
of the island, knowing there was a wailing base on the opposite coast where they could

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:46.680
summon help.

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:52.840
This team of Royal Marines is retracing the steps of that journey in tribute to Shackleton

00:31:52.840 --> 00:31:54.240
and his men.

00:31:54.240 --> 00:32:00.680
But for all their efforts, they can't exactly copy the great walk because the ice is not

00:32:00.680 --> 00:32:03.440
as it was.

00:32:03.440 --> 00:32:11.040
A number of South Georgia's glaciers were photographed by Shackleton's cameraman.

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:34.800
Frozen planets saw a dramatic change when they returned 94 years later.

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:40.080
Most of South Georgia's glaciers have shrunk since Shackleton's time, and most of that

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:44.520
has happened since I first went to the Antarctic 30 years ago.

00:32:44.520 --> 00:32:50.400
I've been to South Georgia several times and seen how greatly the glaciers there have

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:56.400
changed.

00:32:56.400 --> 00:33:02.200
This photograph of a glacier reaching right down to the sea was taken just six years before

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:05.960
I first visited in 1981.

00:33:05.960 --> 00:33:16.960
Now that glacier has retreated by 400 meters away from the beach.

00:33:16.960 --> 00:33:21.480
Temperatures in South Georgia have risen sharply, but the southern hemisphere's most dramatic

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:26.500
warming has happened a little further south.

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:31.520
In recent years, stronger winds blowing over the southern ocean have brought warmer air

00:33:31.520 --> 00:33:44.560
to the 800-mile-long finger of land that forms the northern extremity of the Antarctic continent.

00:33:44.560 --> 00:33:49.840
Here on the Antarctic Peninsula, the changing wind patterns have driven temperatures up

00:33:49.840 --> 00:33:55.880
by nearly 3 degrees centigrade over the last 50 years, 10 times the average rate of the

00:33:55.880 --> 00:34:03.880
rest of the planet.

00:34:03.880 --> 00:34:23.720
The rapid warming is having a big effect on the bird life.

00:34:23.720 --> 00:34:31.280
The Adelie penguin is the most southerly nesting of all penguins.

00:34:31.280 --> 00:34:39.400
And like the polar bear up in the north, their lives are dependent on the sea ice.

00:34:39.400 --> 00:34:43.440
Adelies spend their whole lives near ice.

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:47.760
These birds have spent the winter feeding at the ice edge, but now it's spring, and

00:34:47.760 --> 00:35:00.800
they've started a long trek over the frozen sea towards land.

00:35:00.800 --> 00:35:05.960
They're heading for areas of exposed rock where they gather to breed in colonies that

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:25.240
can be over 100,000 strong.

00:35:25.240 --> 00:35:33.120
But it seems that Adelies don't find the conditions on the peninsula to their liking anymore.

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:38.960
Seventeen years ago, when I was last in the Antarctic, there were large colonies of Adelie

00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:43.200
penguins all along the Antarctic Peninsula.

00:35:43.200 --> 00:36:00.580
Now warming temperatures have meant less sea ice, and Adelie penguin numbers are in decline.

00:36:00.580 --> 00:36:07.560
Many colonies have been empty and fast.

00:36:07.560 --> 00:36:12.640
It may be that penguins are starving, or it may be that they're heading south to colder

00:36:12.640 --> 00:36:25.280
climes where there's still plenty of ice on the sea.

00:36:25.280 --> 00:36:31.080
And as in the Arctic, while ice-loving animals are feeling the heat, animals that like it

00:36:31.080 --> 00:36:41.480
a bit more cozy are moving in.

00:36:41.480 --> 00:36:46.760
The bright orange beaks of Gentoo penguins are a much more common sight on the peninsula

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:47.760
these days.

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:54.200
I always used to know them as residents of the slightly warmer islands north of the Antarctic,

00:36:54.200 --> 00:36:56.800
but they've moved south in numbers.

00:36:56.800 --> 00:37:12.560
There are thought to be 10 times more Gentoos on the peninsula now than just 30 years ago.

00:37:12.560 --> 00:37:23.040
The peninsula has warmed a great deal, but the same is not true further south.

00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:29.640
The Antarctic continent is smothered by the world's greatest ice sheet, one and a half

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:36.600
times the size of Australia and up to three miles thick.

00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:46.960
A staggering 75% of the Earth's fresh water is locked up in this ice.

00:37:46.960 --> 00:37:56.440
Global sea levels would rise by some 60 metres if all this was to melt.

00:37:56.440 --> 00:38:01.920
But what chance is there of that happening here in the coldest, most hostile place on

00:38:01.920 --> 00:38:08.360
Earth?

00:38:08.360 --> 00:38:16.040
The ice beneath me up here on top of the ice cap is so thick that I am short of breath

00:38:16.040 --> 00:38:18.880
simply because of the altitude.

00:38:18.880 --> 00:38:27.800
This is midsummer and the average temperature is some 20 degrees below freezing.

00:38:27.800 --> 00:38:32.080
I can tell you it feels much lower than that.

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:39.960
And even the worst predictions don't suggest that the air is going to warm enough to melt

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:40.960
the ice.

00:38:40.960 --> 00:38:47.240
But now scientists are asking a different question.

00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:52.440
Could the speed at which the Antarctic ice flows off the land be increased by a warmer

00:38:52.440 --> 00:38:55.000
ocean?

00:38:55.000 --> 00:39:13.600
Where the ice sheet meets the sea, scientists are going to extreme lengths to find out.

00:39:13.600 --> 00:39:17.040
Andy Smith works for the British Antarctic Survey.

00:39:17.040 --> 00:39:22.160
What we have here is a one kilogram of pentite explosive.

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:27.480
We're going to use this to generate a shockwave and record the echoes that come back from

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:33.480
underneath the ice.

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:40.240
Andy is particularly interested in mapping the underside of the ice around the coast.

00:39:40.240 --> 00:39:43.680
Because here it isn't resting on land.

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:45.840
It's floating on seawater.

00:39:45.840 --> 00:39:53.520
So if sea temperatures rise just a little, it can be melted from below.

00:39:53.520 --> 00:39:58.440
Around the coast of Antarctica, the glaciers have flowed out across the sea to form immense

00:39:58.440 --> 00:40:07.640
masses of floating freshwater ice called ice shelves.

00:40:07.640 --> 00:40:13.220
These freeze to the land around them, sticking fast and acting like bath plugs, holding back

00:40:13.220 --> 00:40:18.840
the flow of the glaciers into the sea.

00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:23.960
On the Antarctic peninsula, a one degree sea temperature rise has helped to break apart

00:40:23.960 --> 00:40:29.280
seven major ice shelves in the last 30 years.

00:40:29.280 --> 00:40:34.800
This is the Larson B ice shelf, three times the size of Greater London breaking apart

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:37.240
in 2002.

00:40:37.240 --> 00:40:45.560
Afterwards, the glaciers it had been holding back started flowing up to six times faster.

00:40:45.560 --> 00:40:55.480
In 2008, a much larger ice shelf at the southern end of the peninsula started to break up.

00:40:55.480 --> 00:41:01.600
It's an enormous event that's never been filmed before.

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:07.640
Andy Smith is flying down the peninsula to study this phenomenon firsthand.

00:41:07.640 --> 00:41:11.800
We're flying to a place called Wilkins Ice Shelf.

00:41:11.800 --> 00:41:17.160
It's an ice shelf that over the last couple of years has showed a very sudden and dramatic

00:41:17.160 --> 00:41:20.920
breakup.

00:41:20.920 --> 00:41:26.240
The Wilkins Ice Shelf is a two hour long flight south from his research base.

00:41:26.240 --> 00:41:32.120
But Andy can start to see the evidence of ice shelf breakup a long way before he gets

00:41:32.120 --> 00:41:33.120
there.

00:41:33.120 --> 00:41:36.400
As we're heading further south, we can see more and more icebergs in the ocean.

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:47.480
And most of the big ones will be ones that have broken off the ice shelves in this area.

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:51.320
Once we cross the mountains, we should be able to see Wilkins Ice Shelf.

00:41:51.320 --> 00:42:06.800
And then it's not far then to the ice front here where it's collapsing.

00:42:06.800 --> 00:42:11.640
As Andy's team reaches their destination, the scale of what's been happening soon becomes

00:42:11.640 --> 00:42:12.640
clear.

00:42:12.640 --> 00:42:17.600
Here, for thousands of years, an area the size of Yorkshire has been covered by a sheet

00:42:17.600 --> 00:42:24.840
of ice 200 metres thick.

00:42:24.840 --> 00:42:43.080
But now over half of that has broken apart.

00:42:43.080 --> 00:42:49.200
Andy has been studying Antarctic ice for 25 years, but even he is blown away by what he's

00:42:49.200 --> 00:42:50.200
seen.

00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:53.200
Now that is pretty awesome.

00:42:53.200 --> 00:42:55.200
That is remarkable.

00:42:55.200 --> 00:42:59.520
The edge of the ice shelf is just kind of disintegrated.

00:42:59.520 --> 00:43:05.760
Some of the big pieces look like they could be a mile or more in size.

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:09.400
It's almost like a sort of a slow motion explosion.

00:43:09.400 --> 00:43:19.880
It all pushes outwards very quickly.

00:43:19.880 --> 00:43:28.960
Every one of these huge icebergs will slowly drift out to sea.

00:43:28.960 --> 00:43:34.000
To study how fast that happens, Andy needs to get closer to the action.

00:43:34.000 --> 00:43:39.760
We're going to look around and see if we can find a place where we can land, but if we

00:43:39.760 --> 00:43:44.240
can, we'll be able to put on an instrument that will help us monitor the big icebergs

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:55.600
that are breaking off as the ice shelf breaks up.

00:43:55.600 --> 00:44:13.520
Landing on an iceberg is another first for Andy's team.

00:44:13.520 --> 00:44:29.520
This satellite transmitter will help to track the continued breakup of this colossal ice

00:44:29.520 --> 00:44:32.720
shelf.

00:44:32.720 --> 00:44:40.680
The remainder of the Wilkins looks set to break apart soon.

00:44:40.680 --> 00:44:46.080
It's the latest ice shelf to disintegrate in a wave that's been traveling southwards,

00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:51.960
playing a major role in the loss of ice from the peninsula.

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:57.760
Next in line and already weakening in places are the ice shelves that hold back Antarctica's

00:44:57.760 --> 00:45:02.160
gigantic continental ice sheet.

00:45:02.160 --> 00:45:07.840
And it would only take a small corner of this to slide into the sea to have major global

00:45:07.840 --> 00:45:12.120
consequences.

00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:17.740
We've only started to see changes in the Arctic and Antarctic recently, so it's hard to predict

00:45:17.740 --> 00:45:21.040
exactly what impact these changes will have.

00:45:21.040 --> 00:45:26.720
But we can see for ourselves that these places are changing and on a scale that is hard to

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:30.520
ignore.

00:45:30.520 --> 00:45:35.320
The poles, north and south, may seem very remote.

00:45:35.320 --> 00:45:41.880
But what is happening here is likely to have a greater effect upon us than any other aspect

00:45:41.880 --> 00:45:44.400
of global warming.

00:45:44.400 --> 00:45:49.560
If the Arctic sea ice continues to disappear, it will drive up the planet's temperature

00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:51.280
more quickly.

00:45:51.280 --> 00:45:56.440
And the melting ice sheets could contribute to a sea level rise of a meter, enough to

00:45:56.440 --> 00:46:04.200
threaten the homes of millions of people around the world's coasts by the end of the century.

00:46:04.200 --> 00:46:09.320
We've seen that the animals are already adapting to these changes.

00:46:09.320 --> 00:46:38.240
But can we respond to what is happening now to the frozen planet?

00:46:39.320 --> 00:46:58.240
The increasing unpredictability of the ice was a big issue for the frozen planet team

00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:08.960
who spent three years working on top of it.

00:47:08.960 --> 00:47:14.640
Whether on sea, land, lake or river, the state of the ice was the first concern for most

00:47:14.640 --> 00:47:21.560
filming crews.

00:47:21.560 --> 00:47:27.080
Unexpected breakups left many a cameraman in need of a swift rescue.

00:47:27.080 --> 00:47:37.600
Sometimes help came by boat and sometimes by air.

00:47:37.600 --> 00:47:46.200
I had a chance to see the changing ice conditions for myself when I visited the North Pole.

00:47:46.200 --> 00:47:51.360
I flew with the team to a temporary camp that is set up every year in the center of the

00:47:51.360 --> 00:48:00.400
frozen Arctic Ocean to support expeditions to the pole.

00:48:00.400 --> 00:48:06.520
I had never visited the North Pole before, so this was a great highlight for me.

00:48:06.520 --> 00:48:11.640
But it was hard going in temperatures of minus 40, so as soon as filming finished we flew

00:48:11.640 --> 00:48:14.280
south.

00:48:14.280 --> 00:48:19.640
Little did we know that we had made it out just in time.

00:48:19.640 --> 00:48:26.280
We got back from the pole camp last night and I just bumped into the Russian commander

00:48:26.280 --> 00:48:28.840
who just heard from the camp.

00:48:28.840 --> 00:48:34.440
And the news is that a little crack which I'd seen in the ice between our tent and the

00:48:34.440 --> 00:48:44.240
airstrip, which is no more than an inch or so wide, has overnight widened to 20 meters.

00:48:44.240 --> 00:48:49.040
Temporary breakups caused by stormy weather and strong winds have happened before, but

00:48:49.040 --> 00:48:58.200
they've been getting more and more frequent over recent years as the ice has got weaker.

00:48:58.200 --> 00:49:02.800
It was only swift action by the staff that prevented a lot of valuable equipment going

00:49:02.800 --> 00:49:11.440
in the drink.

00:49:11.440 --> 00:49:16.900
The biggest concern was that the ice airstrip might break apart, but luckily it held and

00:49:16.900 --> 00:49:25.220
everyone was able to evacuate when the weather improved.

00:49:25.220 --> 00:49:30.460
The frozen planet team's clearest demonstration of the power and unpredictability of breaking

00:49:30.460 --> 00:49:35.880
ice came when they went to film the melting of a frozen Canadian river.

00:49:35.880 --> 00:49:40.520
Producer Mark Linfield and researcher Matt Swarbrick have travelled to the far north

00:49:40.520 --> 00:49:41.520
of Canada.

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:43.520
Matt, when was the last time you saw a calf?

00:49:43.520 --> 00:49:48.240
I think about three hours ago.

00:49:48.240 --> 00:49:52.320
They've driven through the vast northwest territory on a mission to film the moment

00:49:52.320 --> 00:49:58.880
when this frozen waterfall breaks apart.

00:49:58.880 --> 00:50:03.720
The breakup, when the frozen river above the waterfall thaws and masses of water start

00:50:03.720 --> 00:50:07.160
to flow again, can be a spectacular event.

00:50:07.160 --> 00:50:11.980
But predicting exactly when it's going to break is the big challenge if Mark and Matt

00:50:11.980 --> 00:50:16.800
want to get the best shots.

00:50:16.800 --> 00:50:19.160
And they're not the only ones who want to know.

00:50:19.160 --> 00:50:23.960
When the waterfall breaks, it can flood the town of Hay River just downstream with millions

00:50:23.960 --> 00:50:27.440
of tons of water and ice.

00:50:27.440 --> 00:50:32.520
Mark is taking advice from the scientist Faye Hicks, who has the job of predicting when

00:50:32.520 --> 00:50:34.200
the ice will break.

00:50:34.200 --> 00:50:38.320
What happens is you get ice jams form upstream and they start to dam up the water and it

00:50:38.320 --> 00:50:42.560
builds and builds and builds and that can let go and that's a much bigger wave of water

00:50:42.560 --> 00:50:45.320
than just the normal flow.

00:50:45.320 --> 00:50:49.680
So it just depends upon how dramatically it unfolds.

00:50:49.680 --> 00:51:04.520
Faye takes her research helicopter to monitor the situation upstream of the waterfall.

00:51:04.520 --> 00:51:10.200
Just 10 miles upriver, the ice is starting to break.

00:51:10.200 --> 00:51:14.840
The locals are concerned because huge amounts of water can build up if these ice chunks

00:51:14.840 --> 00:51:25.000
dam the river and that can lead to devastating flooding in the town when the dams burst.

00:51:25.000 --> 00:51:29.400
Using cameras and sonar to assess the state of the river, Faye makes her best guess of

00:51:29.400 --> 00:51:37.760
when this break up will hit the waterfall just above the town.

00:51:37.760 --> 00:51:45.400
Faye's prediction of the 24th of April is exciting news for the team.

00:51:45.400 --> 00:51:50.520
Upstream from here it's already starting to melt and Faye thinks that we may only have

00:51:50.520 --> 00:51:56.080
another one or two days before this whole thing goes, which is almost impossible to

00:51:56.080 --> 00:52:00.200
imagine looking at it now, but that's what she says.

00:52:00.200 --> 00:52:07.720
With the break up seemingly imminent, the team set up their cameras in anticipation.

00:52:07.720 --> 00:52:12.080
With the next 48 hours the weather warms to well above freezing, but there's no sign

00:52:12.080 --> 00:52:13.200
of the break up.

00:52:13.200 --> 00:52:26.160
The team waits and waits and waits.

00:52:26.160 --> 00:52:31.080
Mark is concerned that the crew have to return home soon, so he heads into town to get the

00:52:31.080 --> 00:52:35.600
advice of long-term resident Red McBrien.

00:52:35.600 --> 00:52:40.720
We just have to live with it and take whatever evasive action we can.

00:52:40.720 --> 00:52:44.000
Red has had 50 years of witnessing the power of the river.

00:52:44.000 --> 00:52:48.640
Well we're hoping that she may break up in two or three days even.

00:52:48.640 --> 00:52:50.720
Oh no, no, no, no, that's too soon.

00:52:50.720 --> 00:53:00.040
Oh no, no, you boys you're looking at seven or eight days before she breaks of any significance

00:53:00.040 --> 00:53:06.520
eh, and if she breaks she can jam and hold up.

00:53:06.520 --> 00:53:14.000
She can be, she'd be down here probably around the fifth or sixth of May.

00:53:14.000 --> 00:53:20.160
The townspeople are on tenterhooks, waiting for the big day, but another week goes by

00:53:20.160 --> 00:53:34.280
before anything starts to happen.

00:53:34.280 --> 00:53:36.320
Finally it seems that things might be happening.

00:53:36.320 --> 00:53:43.440
We've just heard some cracks from upstream, so if we're lucky we might get some action.

00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:45.560
Seven o'clock which gives us two hours light.

00:53:45.560 --> 00:53:46.560
Two hours light.

00:53:46.560 --> 00:53:51.920
If that happens at night we're going to miss the whole thing.

00:53:51.920 --> 00:53:56.960
Sure enough the town is put on red alert that the river is about to break in the middle

00:53:56.960 --> 00:53:59.680
of the night.

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:02.600
They've just called a full evacuation of the iron where we're staying.

00:54:02.600 --> 00:54:06.480
If we don't move now we're all going to be underwater and possibly get trapped here for

00:54:06.480 --> 00:54:08.560
a few days.

00:54:08.560 --> 00:54:13.320
The team have to move out and get up to the waterfall, hoping that it doesn't break before

00:54:13.320 --> 00:54:20.640
it's light enough to film.

00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:23.640
Luckily the sun is up before the main event begins.

00:54:23.640 --> 00:54:39.040
That is a serious amount of ice coming around the corner.

00:54:39.040 --> 00:54:44.880
After weeks of waiting, sleeping giant of a river, we thought nothing was going to happen

00:54:44.880 --> 00:54:47.880
and suddenly look at this.

00:54:47.880 --> 00:54:50.880
This is what we're here for.

00:54:50.880 --> 00:54:51.880
Unbelievable.

00:54:51.880 --> 00:54:52.880
Absolutely unbelievable.

00:54:52.880 --> 00:54:53.880
Holy ****.

00:54:53.880 --> 00:54:58.520
How hard is it to get on that thing?

00:54:58.520 --> 00:55:03.280
The team is used to handling multiple cameras, but they don't usually have to dodge 10 ton

00:55:03.280 --> 00:55:05.280
ice flows at the same time.

00:55:05.280 --> 00:55:11.000
You can see it's racing over at an unbelievable speed.

00:55:11.000 --> 00:55:16.320
The power, if you were here to feel this, just the deep rumbling sound of the river,

00:55:16.320 --> 00:55:17.320
I can feel it up to my feet.

00:55:17.320 --> 00:55:18.320
The power, I just can't imagine.

00:55:18.320 --> 00:55:23.800
It had to crush a house in no time.

00:55:23.800 --> 00:55:27.840
The team takes to the air to witness the destruction that's unleashed.

00:55:27.840 --> 00:55:33.840
Huge ice blocks are pushed downstream on the wave of water released by the breaking waterfall.

00:55:33.840 --> 00:55:44.520
This could devastate the town.

00:55:44.520 --> 00:55:47.480
But this year, the townspeople's luck is in.

00:55:47.480 --> 00:55:51.760
The town has escaped flooding.

00:55:51.760 --> 00:55:57.520
Crucially, the ice blocks did not dam the river.

00:55:57.520 --> 00:55:59.200
It's running free.

00:55:59.200 --> 00:56:00.920
And the date of the breakup?

00:56:00.920 --> 00:56:02.640
6th of May.

00:56:02.640 --> 00:56:05.880
The Reds got it right again.

00:56:05.880 --> 00:56:10.760
I don't use any of these gauges and mechanical assistance.

00:56:10.760 --> 00:56:15.560
I just go by what I see on the river as I walk it down.

00:56:15.560 --> 00:56:21.640
And I say I walk it down, I back and forth every day on the river to see what's happening.

00:56:21.640 --> 00:56:27.120
And from that I gauge when it's going to hit here and what the situation is going to be

00:56:27.120 --> 00:56:28.640
like when it does get here.

00:56:28.640 --> 00:56:33.560
You know when it went this morning, I said to my students, guess what the date is.

00:56:33.560 --> 00:56:34.920
Red told us it's the 6th of May.

00:56:34.920 --> 00:56:38.520
And we were, you know, I'm not surprised because we've been here a couple times and that's

00:56:38.520 --> 00:56:39.520
happened.

00:56:39.520 --> 00:56:43.040
10 days, two weeks out, he just looks around and goes, 5th of May.

00:56:43.040 --> 00:56:45.000
And we, how does he know that?

00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:46.000
It's incredible.

00:56:46.000 --> 00:56:53.000
It's because he just has lived on this river and lived this breakup for 50 years.

00:56:53.000 --> 00:56:57.120
Ice scientists are improving the accuracy of their predictions all the time.

00:56:57.120 --> 00:57:01.680
And in the meantime, the people of Hay River have a remarkable guardian.

00:57:01.680 --> 00:57:04.400
Red, you were completely right this year.

00:57:04.400 --> 00:57:06.120
Are you right every year?

00:57:06.120 --> 00:57:08.760
No, I'm...

00:57:08.760 --> 00:57:11.520
I missed the odd one.

00:57:11.520 --> 00:57:12.520
Yes.

00:57:12.520 --> 00:57:30.920
1985, I missed it.

00:57:30.920 --> 00:57:47.540
pains.
